9. NEW VOID ELIMINATION RIPPLE EFFECT

9.1 Objective

Whenever a fighter is eliminated by the Void, the Void itself must visibly react at the precise point where the fighter crosses its elimination boundary.

Think of dropping an object into water and watching the water react with expanding, energetic ripples.

However, the effect should use ALVA’s existing black-and-red Void aesthetic rather than literally converting the Void into blue water.

This effect applies to all stages, not only Adamaris.

9.2 Existing implementation

Inspect:

* js/game/arena.js
* js/game/rendering/hit-fx.js
* js/stages/stage-theme.js

Currently, Arena.checkVoid() detects eliminations and triggers HitEffects.addElimination().

The existing elimination effect includes a fighter-colored burst, flash, particles, and an expanding ring.

Keep that existing elimination burst.

The new Void ripple is an additional effect showing that the Void itself has been disturbed.

It must not simply replace the existing fighter-colored effect with another generic ring.

9.3 Ripple appearance

The new ripple must be vibrant, energetic, and visually substantial.

On elimination:

1. The Void boundary visibly reacts around the contact point.
2. A bright, localized pulse appears.
3. Several concentric ripples expand outward.
4. The nearby black surface appears disturbed.
5. The red boundary rim becomes more intense locally.
6. Wave energy travels outward from the impact.
7. The disturbance loses strength gradually and fades.

The ripples should feel as though they originate from the Void’s surface rather than floating as unrelated particles in front of the screen.

Use layered rings, bright highlights, and animated wave deformation.

The effect should have enough contrast and scale to be unmistakable during fast gameplay.

It must not appear as a weak, nearly invisible outline.

9.4 Actual Void-surface reaction

This is the most important visual requirement.

The Void must genuinely appear to react to the elimination.

Do not implement only a circular particle animation over a completely unchanged black Void.

Add a localized, temporary disturbance to the Void’s rendered boundary or surface.

For example:

* Create an impact centered on the boundary crossing.
* Calculate an expanding disturbance around that position.
* Temporarily increase local wave amplitude.
* Allow that disturbance to propagate along the boundary.
* Make the nearby red rim ripple along with the black edge.
* Gradually reduce the disturbance until the Void returns to normal.

A damped wave impulse or similar procedural animation would be appropriate.

Keep the original organic wave behavior as the baseline.

The new impulse should be layered over it.

The Void’s shape should visibly change, but the gameplay kill boundary must never move because of the effect.

The disturbance is visual only.

9.5 Exact elimination position

Calculate the visual impact position from the fighter’s actual crossing of the Void boundary.

Support eliminations through:

* The left Void boundary.
* The right Void boundary.
* The upper Void boundary.
* The lower Void boundary.
* Corners or diagonal crossings.

Use the crossing side and world-space position to anchor the effect to the Void.

Do not place every ripple in the middle of the screen.

Do not use a fixed stage position.

Do not spawn the ripple at the fighter’s original spawn point.

Use the fighter’s previous and current positions where necessary to identify the actual boundary crossing, rather than depending on an imprecise screen-space approximation.

The effect must remain correctly positioned while the camera moves.

It must also work when the Void is closing during overtime.

9.6 Multiple eliminations

Support multiple simultaneous or closely spaced eliminations.

Each elimination creates its own ripple.

Ripple effects may overlap naturally.

Do not have a second elimination unexpectedly erase the first ripple.

Effects should expire cleanly, without growing arrays indefinitely.

The effect must work for either fighter and must not depend on character-specific code.

9.7 Visual synchronization

Coordinate the new ripple with the existing elimination burst.

The expected sequence:

* Fighter crosses the Void boundary.
* Fighter is marked eliminated through the existing rules.
* The Void reacts immediately at that location.
* The fighter-colored burst appears over the reaction.
* Ripples spread and fade.
* Normal gameplay continues or the match transitions to its result.

The ripple must not delay elimination, change scoring, prevent respawning, or interfere with other combat effects.

9.8 Reduced motion

Respect ALVA’s existing reduced-motion behavior.

In reduced-motion mode:

* Keep a clearly visible localized elimination pulse.
* Reduce or eliminate aggressive traveling distortions.
* Disable unnecessary screen shake.
* Preserve the recognizable reaction without intense animation.

Normal mode should show the full vibrant effect.

